WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Speaker 1: We worried that this would happen and&nbsp;
it's happening. COVID anti-vaccine sentiment is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.340
now bleeding over into other vaccines, and&nbsp;
parents are increasingly hesitant to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.140
their kids vaccinated against all sorts of&nbsp;
other things, including measles. And one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:22.140
those. And one of the problems with that&nbsp;
is we are now starting to see outbreaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:29.160
exactly as predicted. You might remember back in&nbsp;
November, we talked about this story. CNN reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.560
as measles outbreak sickens more than a dozen&nbsp;
children in Ohio. Local health officials seek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.780
help from the CDC. A growing measles outbreak&nbsp;
in Columbus has sickened more than a dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:46.920
unvaccinated children and hospitalized nine of&nbsp;
them. Look at that ratio, 75% hospitalization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:54.360
We often forget how serious measles is,&nbsp;
particularly in kids, because vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.740
is so effective. And this leads to some parents&nbsp;
saying, who ever heard of a kid getting measles?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.200
It doesn't sound like it's likely and it certainly&nbsp;
doesn't sound serious. That's because of vaccines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:15.120
We have a report now from NBC4 about how vaccine&nbsp;
hesitancy that has sort of spilled over from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.400
COVID vaccines into other vaccines is causing&nbsp;
exactly this problem. Take a listen to this.&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.540
Speaker 2: Hey, everybody. US childhood&nbsp;
vaccinations have dipped again this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:30.300
year. A new report from the CDC suggests. What&nbsp;
started as a concern about the COVID 19 vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.640
especially in younger people, is now carrying&nbsp;
over into other shots, too, including polio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.220
diphtheria and measles. It's likely one of the&nbsp;
reasons cluster outbreaks of diseases such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:48.120
measles are on the rise. CDC reports show last&nbsp;
year it was in the Twin Cities Minnesota area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.040
Currently, it's in and around Columbus, Ohio.
Speaker 3: In the Columbus outbreak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.200
there were over 85 cases and can be counting.&nbsp;
That was compared to last week's data. But over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.929
30 of those kids were hospitalized and most&nbsp;
of those cases were in unvaccinated children.&nbsp;

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:07.260
Speaker 2: Yeah, Dr. Drew, his work is a&nbsp;
pediatrician who told me this new report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:14.460
shows overall those receiving vaccines required&nbsp;
by most states is now at about 93%. It was at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.820
95% just two years ago before the pandemic.
Speaker 1: Now that 93 and 95% number is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:28.500
critically important. The herd immunity, measles&nbsp;
is so contagious that the herd immunity threshold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:36.180
to prevent outbreaks is considered to be 95%.&nbsp;
That is dropped to 93% when it comes to children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:43.440
entering kindergarten with with the required&nbsp;
immunizations, 93%. When you are below, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.520
people think, oh, this is some vague thing. It's&nbsp;
just numbers. No, it has real world consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.160
when you go below that herd immunity threshold for&nbsp;
some diseases. You will start to see outbreaks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:03:00.840
And that is exactly what is going on. And this&nbsp;
was the fear that the COVID anti-vaxxers who first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:10.740
entered Anti-vaxxer ism with the COVID vaccine&nbsp;
would bleed over into more general opposition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:17.760
child vaccinations because the general environment&nbsp;
starts to become one of vaccine hesitancy. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.680
if you get enough people in a community that&nbsp;
are skeptical or opposed to vaccines, it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.420
make others in the community more likely to say,&nbsp;
well, if these three families aren't doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:33.480
maybe I shouldn't do it either. If the health&nbsp;
care providers, if the public health officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.440
are well prepared to deal with the vaccine&nbsp;
hesitancy, they are going to be under-resourced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.000
and not able to keep those vaccine rates up.&nbsp;
And I've spoken to a number of pediatricians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.060
I know friends of mine who say I get more people&nbsp;
than ever, even in liberal areas, people coming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:56.280
me and saying, you know, does my child really&nbsp;
need all these vaccines? Some of these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.140
don't really seem super serious or they don't seem&nbsp;
super common or likely that my child would get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.640
And the pediatricians have to explain,&nbsp;
well, that's because of the success and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.560
effectiveness of many of these vaccines. This is a&nbsp;
separate conversation from the COVID vaccine. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:18.270
unfortunately, what we expected to see happen is&nbsp;
indeed happening as we see in these reports. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:23.040
will turn it around? I quite frankly, don't know.&nbsp;
It's pretty horrifying, to be totally honest.

